            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Heisenberg and the Copenhagen Interpretation</strong><br>\nIn the late 1920s, Heisenberg worked with Niels Bohr in Copenhagen to develop the Copenhagen interpretation of quantum mechanics. This theory furthered the concept that particles do not have definite properties until they are measured. Their collaboration was crucial to the advancement of modern physics, cementing both scientists' legacies as pioneers in the field.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Quantum Field Theory</strong><br>\nApart from his work on quantum mechanics, Heisenberg also made significant contributions to quantum field theory. He was interested in unifying the theories of quantum mechanics with Einstein's theory of relativity. While he did not achieve this goal during his lifetime, his work paved the way for later physicists to continue exploring this integration.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Heisenberg During World War II</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Heisenberg remained in Germany and became a leading figure in the German nuclear weapon project. His role in this project has been a subject of much debate. After the war, Heisenberg claimed he had never intended to build a bomb for the Nazis, and his actions during the war remain a topic of historical inquiry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
