          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Career and Politics</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Heisenberg was appointed director of the Max Planck Institute for Physics, which relocated to Munich in 1958. He continued his research on quantum physics and played a role in the rebuilding of scientific research in Germany. Heisenberg also engaged in the politics of science, advocating for peaceful uses of atomic energy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Awards</strong><br>\nHeisenberg received numerous awards for his contributions to physics, including the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1932 for the creation of quantum mechanics. His work not only influenced theoretical physics but also had practical implications for the development of technologies based on quantum principles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nHeisenberg continued to work and teach in the field of physics until his death on February 1, 1976. His legacy is one of intellectual bravery and profound discovery. Heisenberg's ideas not only shaped the course of twentieth-century physics but also continue to influence physics today. As an individual, his curiosity and commitment to science exemplify the virtues of a great scientist.</p>\n"
              },
